WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:06.060
Imagine you run a simple client program.

2
00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:10.330
Almost instantly, it reaches across the
network and establishes a successful

3
00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:13.940
connection. But over on the
server machine, there is a delay.

4
00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:18.230
The server's code is still initializing
and hasn't even reached the line of code

5
00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:20.260
meant to accept new connections.

6
00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:26.210
So, if the server application hasn't
explicitly accepted the connection yet, who or

7
00:00:26.260 --> 00:00:28.580
what actually answered the call?

8
00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:34.130
To solve this timing mystery, we have to
look at how networking operates in a hidden

9
00:00:34.180 --> 00:00:38.720
layer of the system, mediated by a
specific endpoint called a socket.

10
00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:41.980
A socket is the endpoint
of a communication.

11
00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:46.580
When a program creates one, the operating
system returns a file descriptor.

12
00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:52.050
You use this descriptor with basic system
calls like read, write, and close exactly

13
00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:55.600
as you would with a pipe.
Sockets come in different types.

14
00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:59.580
A connectionless protocol like
UDP sends independent datagrams.

15
00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:01.920
It behaves like sending a telegram.

16
00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:05.480
Data can be lost, duplicated,
or arrive out of order.

17
00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:07.260
And neither side gets an error.

18
00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:11.520
Then there is TCP. This
is a connected protocol.

19
00:01:11.800 --> 00:01:16.350
Once the connection is set up, it guarantees
a reliable, ordered byte stream between

20
00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:20.890
the two machines. Since our opening
mystery involves a successful, guaranteed

21
00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:23.900
connection, these machines are using TCP.

22
00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:28.170
A formal connection setup happened behind
the scenes, completely independent of the

23
00:01:28.220 --> 00:01:32.780
server application. Let's look at the
structure of the operating system.

24
00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:36.940
The server process runs in user
mode, above the privilege boundary.

25
00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:41.380
The core of the system, the kernel,
operates in the restricted space below.

26
00:01:41.740 --> 00:01:45.350
To start, the server process
creates a socket and calls bind.

27
00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:50.130
This pushes a request down to the kernel
to assign that socket a specific network

28
00:01:50.180 --> 00:01:54.600
address and a local port. But
there is a hardware conflict here.

29
00:01:55.020 --> 00:02:00.470
An x86-64 processor stores numbers in
little-endian order, reading bytes back to

30
00:02:00.520 --> 00:02:04.880
front. The network protocol
strictly requires big-endian order.

31
00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:10.270
If a programmer forgets to use the htons
function to translate the bytes, a severe

32
00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:17.190
bug occurs. The processor reverses the
bytes of port 7000, which is hex 1B58, and

33
00:02:17.240 --> 00:02:21.300
the server accidentally
binds to port 22555 instead.

34
00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:26.310
This mandatory data translation shows that
the application relies entirely on the

35
00:02:26.360 --> 00:02:30.890
kernel's network stack. It must format its
requests to match the exact standards the

36
00:02:30.940 --> 00:02:35.950
kernel expects. Once the address is properly
bound, the server application calls

37
00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:40.630
listen. This sets the socket to a passive
state, signaling the kernel to monitor

38
00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:42.600
that port for incoming traffic.

39
00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:46.990
Over on the remote machine, the client
application creates its own socket and sends

40
00:02:47.040 --> 00:02:49.800
a connect request targeting
the server's address.

41
00:02:50.120 --> 00:02:52.460
Now we return to our initial mystery.

42
00:02:52.700 --> 00:02:56.270
The server's kernel receives this
request below the privilege boundary.

43
00:02:56.320 --> 00:02:58.840
It does not wake up the
server application yet.

44
00:02:58.940 --> 00:03:01.370
Instead, the two kernels
communicate directly.

45
00:03:01.420 --> 00:03:04.520
They perform the TCP 3-way handshake.

46
00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:09.280
SYN from the client, SYN-ACK from the
server, and a final ACK from the client.

47
00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:13.390
Once finished, the server's kernel places
the established connection into an accept

48
00:03:13.440 --> 00:03:17.800
queue. When the server application
is finally ready, it calls accept.

49
00:03:17.960 --> 00:03:20.670
It does not execute the
network handshake itself.

50
00:03:20.720 --> 00:03:23.620
It simply pulls that waiting
connection out of the queue.

51
00:03:23.860 --> 00:03:28.110
The accept function takes that established
connection and returns a brand new file

52
00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:32.810
descriptor. This new descriptor is dedicated
exclusively to talking to that specific

53
00:03:32.860 --> 00:03:36.400
client, while the original socket
keeps listening for others.

54
00:03:36.840 --> 00:03:40.740
When the communication ends, closing
the connection is a symmetric process.

55
00:03:40.920 --> 00:03:43.580
It begins the moment
either side calls close.

56
00:03:43.960 --> 00:03:48.030
The system that initiates the close goes
through a specific sequence and enters a

57
00:03:48.080 --> 00:03:49.760
state called TIME_WAIT.

58
00:03:49.900 --> 00:03:54.150
This acts as a safety buffer to catch
any stray, delayed network packets still

59
00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:55.310
traveling across the internet.

60
00:03:55.360 --> 00:04:00.590
On Linux, this specific TIME_WAIT entry
survives in the kernel for 60 seconds

61
00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:02.550
after the program itself ends.

62
00:04:02.600 --> 00:04:05.380
This causes a common development problem.

63
00:04:05.620 --> 00:04:11.150
If you stop the server code and restart it
immediately, it crashes with an EADDRINUSE

64
00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:15.920
error. The port fails to bind because
the kernel still has it locked.

65
00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:21.590
To solve this, the programmer must
explicitly set the SO_REUSEADDR flag before

66
00:04:21.640 --> 00:04:25.520
binding. This proves the
overarching rule of sockets.

67
00:04:25.840 --> 00:04:30.070
From the first handshake to the final
close, the kernel controls the lifecycle of

68
00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:32.280
the port. Not the application.

69
00:04:33.220 --> 00:04:37.150
Question one. What function returns a
new file descriptor for an established

70
00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:40.600
connection? The function
that does this is accept.

71
00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:45.640
Question two. Which layer completes
the TCP three-way handshake?

72
00:04:45.800 --> 00:04:47.580
The application or the kernel?

73
00:04:47.980 --> 00:04:50.460
The layer that completes it is the kernel.

74
00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:56.260
Now open animation A8.1 in the
course hub and run lab TD7.
